A||||4||Gokart||Will anything seize?||||5
Z||000000||Gokart||10-23-1999||06:11 AM||||I started getting my winter car ready last weekend and dicovered the alternator is seized. That bites cause I didn't want to put any more money into it. It makes me concerned about my GT. I'm going to put her up on blocks to prevent the tires from getting flat spots but was wondering if anything could possibly seize while she's resting for about 4 months?||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||10-23-1999||07:41 AM||||When I put my streetrods up for the winter, I make sure the antifreeze is ok and then pull the batteries out and set them on a plank of wood down in the basement. Then at least once a month I use a charger on them. It shortens a battery's life span if it sits out in freezing temps and is not used or charged up regularly.<P>Also, if the weather gets nice enough, I'll stick the batteries back in the cars a few times during the winter and fire them up to lube the engines and to prevent the seals from drying out. The longer a car sits without running, the more that can go wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-23-1999||04:04 PM||||you don't need to block the car really. If the tires take a flat spot it will come out quickly as soon as you drive it and you really want to drive it on good days. leaving a car sit for months will always screw things up. Flat spotting was/is more a problem with tires that used Rayon or More recently Kevlar. Rayon is not used anymore. Nylon and other compounds are stronger.<P>Like what else?????????<P>brake rotors build up rust if not used fairly regular. to much rust means a sure brake job.<P>seals go bad. Todays oils, especiallty ATF, contain aditives that preserve the seals. you need to stir them up every so often.<P>Gas. Make sure you but StaBil or some similar storage agent in the gas and run the car for awhile to mix it everywhere.<P>Cover the grate in front of the windscreen so things don't settly in.<P>plug the engine air intake, and exhaust so vermin don't make homes in there.<P>Try to run the car at least once a month. Drive it at least that often if you can.<BR>when you run the car let it run for at least 30 minutes. preferably longer. you want to drive off all the moisture and that means heating the engine to full operating temp and keeping it there awhile.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AL||10-23-1999||05:42 PM||||i have seen cars parked for 5 or 6 yrs,and i've never seen one blow a seal they always seem to fire right up and then we changed the oil.but for acar to sit for only 4 months that's nothing, justdisconnect battery , pull it out and let it sit,as far as rust on rotors well as soon as you start using the car they find a way of cleaning them selves up.  if you see rust and want to clean it a little sand paper will take it off, rotors don't go bad sitting, they go bad when you glaze them up and never cut them when you change your brakes, and that's when worpage begins.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||10-25-1999||09:28 AM||||That reminds me of a funny story.  I knew I a guy (the second cousin of a friend) with a full fledged rice burner. (Miata)  He was detailing the car, and decided that WD-40 would shine the rims up nicely.  When he got down to look at the rims, he saw his tarnished brake rotors, so he <B>sprayed those too!</B>.  So when he finished he was going to take the car for a spin and when he pressed the brakes, nothing happened.   He managed to get the car stopped somehow before hitting a 2 story concrete side wall of a machine shop.<P>I think next time he just bought some foaming wheel cleaner and called it a day.||||reg||1
